# Line endings are correctness here, not style.
#
# cmd.exe mis-parses a batch file that has LF-only line endings. Labels and %~ parameter
# expansion break, and the errors name nothing useful:
#   'the' is not recognized as an internal or external command
#   The following usage of the path operator in batch-parameter substitution is invalid: %~fI
# So .cmd must arrive as CRLF on every machine, whatever a contributor's core.autocrlf
# happens to be set to - including a checkout made on macOS and shared with Windows.
#
# Shell scripts need the opposite. A trailing CR on the shebang makes the kernel look for
# an interpreter literally named "bash\r", which fails with "no such file or directory"
# while pointing at a file that plainly exists.
#
# .ps1 is deliberately absent: PowerShell reads either ending on both hosts, so pinning
# it would only churn every script in the repo.

* text=auto
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.sh  text eol=lf
